{%- comment -%}
  A legal document — the privacy policy, and the shape the cookie policy and
  terms of use take too.
  ==========================================================================
  A reading instrument, not a landing page: no hero image, no figures, no
  marquee. The whole page is one section, because a clause is not a section
  an editor should be able to drag out from under its own numbering.

  The clause NUMBER is not stored. It is the position in the list, so
  deleting clause 3 renumbers 4..12 without anyone touching them — the one
  thing a hand-numbered legal document always gets wrong.

  THE INDEX IS GENERATED from the same list, so a clause cannot exist
  without a link to it, and a link cannot point at a clause that is gone.
  `index_label` is separate from the heading on purpose: upstream shortens
  "Collection of Personal Information and its use" to "Collection & Use of
  PII" in the rail, and the rail is narrow.

  js/ondc/doc-index.js only toggles a class on those links. It never creates
  a node — Bookshop replaces this section's DOM while an editor types.
{%- endcomment -%}
<section class="bk-legal-head">
  {% if eyebrow != blank %}<div class="bk-eyebrow">{{ eyebrow }}</div>{% endif %}
  <h1 data-bk-intro>{{ heading }}{% if heading_accent != blank %} <em>{{ heading_accent }}</em>{% endif %}</h1>
  {% if sub != blank %}<p class="bk-hero-sub" data-bk-scrub>{{ sub }}</p>{% endif %}
  {%- comment -%}
    A <div> where upstream writes a <p>: the field is rich text, so whatever
    an editor types arrives wrapped in its own <p>, and a <p> inside a <p> is
    closed by the parser before the links are reached. `.bk-note` has no
    rule that depends on the tag. The margin guard is in legal-doc.css.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% if read_alongside != blank %}<div class="bk-note">{{ read_alongside }}</div>{% endif %}
</section>

{%- comment -%}
    Without the index the body is one centred column, and how wide that
    column is depends on the document: `--bk-legal-measure` is a custom
    property beckn.css already defaults to .62fr, so an empty field is
    upstream's default and the cookie policy's .58fr is its own choice.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <section class="bk-legal-body{% unless show_index %} bk-legal-body-solo{% endunless %}"{% if measure != blank %} style="--bk-legal-measure: {{ measure }}"{% endif %} aria-label="{{ body_label }}">

  {% if show_index %}
    <aside class="bk-doc-index" aria-label="{{ index_aria | default: "On this page" }}">
      {% for clause in clauses %}<a class="bk-doc-index-link" href="#{{ clause.clause_anchor }}" data-bk-doc-link>{{ clause.index_label | default: clause.clause_title }}</a>{% endfor %}
    </aside>
  {% endif %}

  {% comment -%}
    The number counts NUMBERED clauses, not loop positions. Terms of Use opens
    with an unnumbered preamble and then runs 01..11, so a clause that opts out
    of the number must not consume one either — otherwise the ledger it prints
    of itself disagrees with the clauses it points at.
  {%- endcomment -%}{%- assign clause_n = 0 -%}
  <div class="bk-clauses">
    {% for clause in clauses %}{%- unless clause.unnumbered -%}{%- assign clause_n = clause_n | plus: 1 -%}{%- endunless %}
      <article class="bk-clause" id="{{ clause.clause_anchor }}" data-bk-clause>
        {% unless clause.unnumbered %}<span class="bk-clause-num" aria-hidden="true">{% if clause_n < 10 %}0{% endif %}{{ clause_n }}</span>
        {% endunless %}<h2>{{ clause.clause_title }}</h2>
        {%- for part in clause.parts -%}
          {%- case part.part_kind -%}
            {%- when "subheading" -%}
              <h3 class="bk-clause-sub">{{ part.subheading }}</h3>
            {%- when "barriers" -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                A <div> where upstream writes a <span>, for the same reason the
                related-documents line is one: the row's text is rich text, and
                an editor's <p> does not belong inside a <span>. `.bk-barrier`
                is a flex row, so a div and a span are the same flex item.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <div class="bk-barriers">{% for row in part.barriers %}<div class="bk-barrier"><span class="bk-barrier-n">{{ row.marker }}</span><div class="bk-barrier-t">{{ row.text_html }}</div></div>{% endfor %}</div>
            {%- when "caps_p" -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                The statutory blocks are set in capitals and `.bk-caps` has to
                sit on the <p> or the <ul> itself — `.bk-clause p` sets an
                explicit font-size, so a wrapper would not win by inheritance.
                No rich-text editor will put a class there, which is why these
                are their own part kinds rather than a tick-box on Text.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <p class="bk-caps">{{ part.caps_text }}</p>
            {%- when "caps_ul" -%}
              <ul class="bk-caps">{% for caps_item in part.caps_items %}<li>{{ caps_item.html }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
            {%- when "clause_index" -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                The ledger Terms of Use prints of itself, in place of the side
                index the other two legal pages carry. Generated from the same
                clause list, with the same count, so a clause cannot be in one
                and missing from the other.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <nav class="bk-clause-toc"{% if part.nav_label != blank %} aria-label="{{ part.nav_label }}"{% endif %}>
                {%- assign toc_n = 0 -%}
                {%- for toc_clause in clauses -%}
                  {%- unless toc_clause.unnumbered -%}
                    {%- assign toc_n = toc_n | plus: 1 -%}
                    <a href="#{{ toc_clause.clause_anchor }}"><b>{% if toc_n < 10 %}0{% endif %}{{ toc_n }}</b>{{ toc_clause.index_label | default: toc_clause.clause_title }}</a>
                  {%- endunless -%}
                {%- endfor -%}
              </nav>
            {%- when "note" -%}
              <div class="bk-clause-note">{% if part.note_title != blank %}<h3 class="bk-clause-sub">{{ part.note_title }}</h3>{% endif %}{{ part.note_html }}</div>
            {%- else -%}
              {{ part.html }}
          {%- endcase -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </article>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

</section>
